{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And you shall answer.</b> It is possible that the priests ask, saying, What is this that you have brought? Therefore 'and you shall answer.' Or it is like 'and he began,' and likewise 'Job answered first.'" "<b>A wandering Aramean.</b> The word אובד is from verbs that are intransitive. If it referred to Laban as an Aramean, Scripture would say מאביד or מאבד. And further, what reason to say 'Laban sought to destroy my father' and 'he went down to Egypt'? Laban had no part in the descent to Egypt. The nearer meaning is that 'Aramean' is Jacob, as if Scripture said: When my father was in Aram, he was אובד. The meaning is destitute, without property; and likewise 'give hire to the אובד.' And witness 'he drinks and forgets his poverty' — and behold, he is an Aramean. 'אובד was my father' — the meaning is that I did not inherit the land from my father, for he was poor when he came to Aram. He was also a stranger in Egypt, and he was there במתי מעט, and afterward he became a great nation. And you, YHWH, brought us out of slavery and gave us a good land. And let no disputant object: how is Jacob called an Aramean? Behold, like it is 'Jether the Ishmaelite,' and he was an Israelite, for so it is written." "<b>במתי.</b> Construct state, for a singular form is not found. ומתים is plural. And Rabbi Jonah analyzed it entirely and explained it well under the entry מתה."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:24:56.055429554Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}